    "items": [
      {
        "id": "def-td-reversible-process",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Reversible thermodynamic process record",
        "statement": "Let $P=(I_P,x_P^-,x_P^+,z_P,\\kappa_P,r_P)$ be an admitted closed process record as defined in [[def-td-process-and-protocol]], with equilibrium endpoints $x_P^-,x_P^+\\in D_c$. The record is **reversible relative to its declared boundary and protocol** when the model also admits a reverse record $P^{\\mathrm{rev}}$ for the same system and material constitution, starting at $x_P^+$ and ending at $x_P^-$, such that every auxiliary and device state specified by the protocol is restored to its initial state and every signed boundary transfer is reversed. In particular,\n\n$$Q[P^{\\mathrm{rev}}]=-Q[P],\\qquad W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P^{\\mathrm{rev}}]=-W_{\\mathrm{on}}[P].$$\n\nIf the protocol records additional transfer channels, their signed values must likewise be negatives of their forward values. A record that is slow is not reversible merely because it is slow; it must satisfy the reverse-record condition above. Reversibility is therefore relative to what the model includes inside the boundary and which reverse controls it admits.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-process-and-protocol"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-process-and-protocol": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.F and IV.A",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, Chapter 4 of the complete author-hosted lecture notes, §§4.3.1–4.3.4",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys4.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§2.5–2.6",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Admitted closed process records with equilibrium endpoints in the nonrelativistic macroscopic thermodynamic framework; reversibility is relative to the declared system, auxiliary-device boundary, and transfer protocol."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-thermal-contact-constraints",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Thermal contact family and constraints",
        "statement": "For each material constitution $c$, let $D_c$ be the equilibrium state domain from [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. A **thermal-contact family** is a model-declared set $\\mathcal T$ of tagged pairs $(c,x)$ with $x\\in D_c$. A **thermal-contact protocol** for two states $x,y\\in\\mathcal T$ is an admitted preparation of their compound system in which the two constituents are separated by a declared diathermal contact: matter transfer and mechanical or composition work across that contact are forbidden, while the protocol permits thermal energy transfer between the constituents. The compound's external boundary and all retained constraints are specified as in [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]] and [[def-td-process-and-protocol]].\n\nThe pair is in **thermal contact equilibrium**, written $x\\sim_T y$, when the admitted contact preparation leaves both constituent coordinates unchanged, is an equilibrium preparation, and permits no net heat transfer between them. The relation $\\sim_T$ is defined only on the stated family $\\mathcal T$ and for the declared contact protocols. This definition does not assert that $\\sim_T$ is reflexive, symmetric, or transitive, and it does not assign a real-valued temperature to its equivalence classes.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
          "def-td-process-and-protocol"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-process-and-protocol": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.F and IV.A",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            },
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, Chapter 4 of the complete author-hosted lecture notes, §4.1",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys4.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §2.2",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Admitted pair contacts between equilibrium macrostates of the nonrelativistic macroscopic thermodynamic framework; no temperature scale or contact transitivity is defined here."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Concave macroscopic fundamental entropy",
        "statement": "For each admitted material constitution $c$, the model adopts a finite scalar function\n\n$$S_c:D_c\\longrightarrow\\mathbb R\\ \\text{with values measured in }\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}},$$\n\non the equilibrium state domain $D_c$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. This framework treats $S_c$ as primitive constitutive data: its existence and form are assumed, not derived from the word entropy, statistical mechanics, or experimental evidence.\n\nOn every specified convex region $C\\subseteq D_c$ in the dimensionless coordinates of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], $S_c$ is continuous and concave, meaning that for all $x,y\\in C$ and every $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]),\n\n$$S_c((1-\\alpha)x+\\alpha y)\\ge (1-\\alpha)S_c(x)+\\alpha S_c(y).$$\n\nThe domain assumptions are local to the material and region named; no unlisted coordinate tuple is presumed physically realizable. The input of $S_c$ is the represented state $\\pi_c(e)$; when the function is written using physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, those coordinates mean the SI values whose dimensionless numerical tuple is $\\pi_c(e)$. For independent separated macroscopic systems $A,B$ admitted by [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], entropy is additive under that postulate's composition map $\\kappa_{AB}$:\n\n$$S_{A\\sqcup B}(\\kappa_{AB}(x_A,x_B))=S_A(x_A)+S_B(x_B).$$\n\nOn each separately designated open, single-phase chart in the physical coordinates $(U,V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r)$, assume the numerical representative of $S_c$ is $C^2$ in the sense of [[def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms]]: its first and second coordinate partial derivatives exist and are continuous. The partial derivative $S_U$ at fixed $V,n_1,\\ldots,n_r$ is the ordinary partial derivative in the $U$ coordinate, as in [[def-directional-and-partial-derivatives]], and is assumed finite and strictly positive. The numerical chart input uses $\\hat U=U/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$; consequently $S_U=(1\\,\\mathrm J)^{-1}\\partial S_c/\\partial\\hat U$ and has units $\\mathrm{K^{-1}}$. The other physical coordinates are similarly represented using the fixed SI units of [[def-td-si-quantities]]. No differentiability, strict concavity, or positive $S_U$ is asserted outside those charts. This item does not itself define a thermometer calibration or claim a temperature law.\n\nFor the tagged bulk family with scaling maps $\\delta_\\lambda$ and domains $B_{c,\\lambda}$ from [[post-td-bulk-additivity]], also adopt, for every admitted $\\lambda>0$ and $x\\in B_{c,\\lambda}$,\n\n$$S_{c^{(\\lambda)}}(\\delta_\\lambda x)=\\lambda S_c(x).$$\n\nThis is a bulk homogeneity assumption for tagged scaled systems, not exact finite-size scaling for every body. Lieb–Yngvason develop a different accessibility-based route in which entropy is represented from additional operational hypotheses; this framework does not claim that their derivation proves the present primitive entropy postulate.",
        "deps": [
          "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms",
          "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives",
          "def-interval",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "def-td-si-quantities",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-ck-euclidean-maps-and-diffeomorphisms": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-directional-and-partial-derivatives": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-interval": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-si-quantities": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, Chapter 4 of the complete author-hosted lecture notes, §§4.3.1–4.3.4",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys4.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§2.5–2.6 and §2.9",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.F and IV.A",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 4,
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "The adopted entropy representation for the declared nonrelativistic, macroscopic, additive bulk thermodynamic systems; smooth derivative conditions apply only on the named single-phase charts."
      },
      {
        "id": "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set",
        "kind": "definition",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Isolated compound and feasible equilibrium set",
        "statement": "Fix a finite list of named subsystems $s_1,\\ldots,s_m$, where $m\\in\\mathbb N$ and $m\\ge1$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), each having its own equilibrium state domain $D_{c_i}$ as defined in [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]]. Their separated endpoint state space is the Cartesian product ([[def-cartesian-product]])\n\n$$\\mathcal D:=D_{c_1}\\times\\cdots\\times D_{c_m}.$$\n\nAn **isolated compound** is the system consisting of those subsystems and any named auxiliaries or work devices included inside a declared outer boundary, with no matter or energy transfer across that outer boundary during the admitted process, in the sense of [[def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges]]. Transfers between constituents are internal to the compound. Its total energy and other extensive coordinates use the composition convention of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]]. The additive entropy sum applies only when the constituent split is admitted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] as a collection of independent separated macroscopic systems; an interacting compound outside that scope needs its own total-entropy model.\n\nFor specified totals and retained barriers, an **equilibrium feasible set** is the model-declared subset $\\mathcal F\\subseteq\\mathcal D$ of endpoint tuples satisfying those totals and exactly the exchange permissions in the declared protocol. Totals are imposed on the dimensioned coordinate maps $U_c,V_c,n_{c,j}$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], not by treating the dimensionless numerical coordinates as quantities with units. For example, if two constituents are in thermal contact through [[def-td-thermal-contact-constraints]] while their volumes and mole amounts remain fixed, $\\mathcal F$ consists of the energy partitions $(x_A,x_B)$ with the prescribed total energy and those fixed volumes and amounts. Allowing volume or species exchange changes $\\mathcal F$ only when the protocol explicitly permits that exchange; retained walls keep the corresponding coordinates fixed.\n\nThe set $\\mathcal F$ may be empty, noncompact, or otherwise fail to contain an entropy-maximizing state. This definition imposes no existence, compactness, convexity, uniqueness, or attainment assumption.",
        "deps": [
          "def-cartesian-product",
          "def-natural-numbers",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
          "def-td-thermal-contact-constraints",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-cartesian-product": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-natural-numbers": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-td-equilibrium-state-domain": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-thermal-contact-constraints": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity": "physical-assumption",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy": "physical-assumption"
        },
        "provenance": {
