          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "David Tong, Statistical Physics, Chapter 4 of the complete author-hosted lecture notes, §§4.1 and 4.3.3",
              "url": "https://davidtong.org/pdfs/teaching/statistical-physics/statphys4.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Daniel Arovas, Lecture Notes on Thermodynamics and Statistical Mechanics, §§2.2, 2.6 and 2.9",
              "url": "https://courses.physics.ucsd.edu/2010/Spring/physics210a/LECTURES/STATMECH.pdf"
            },
            {
              "title": "Lieb and Yngvason, The Physics and Mathematics of the Second Law of Thermodynamics, §§II.F and IV.A",
              "url": "https://arxiv.org/pdf/cond-mat/9708200v2"
            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 5,
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Feasible endpoint macrostates for a declared isolated compound of nonrelativistic macroscopic thermodynamic systems; only explicitly permitted exchanges and retained constraints are included."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-entropy-maximum",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Equilibrium as an attained global entropy maximum",
        "statement": "Let $\\mathcal F$ be the explicitly declared feasible endpoint set of an isolated compound whose constituents are independent separated macroscopic systems, as defined in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. For this route, adopt the physical criterion that any endpoint tuple the model classifies as a stable equilibrium under these fixed totals, retained partitions, and permitted exchanges satisfies\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})=\\max_{x\\in\\mathcal F}S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x),\\qquad S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}S_{c_i}(x_i),$$\n\nHere $\\max$ means that $x^{\\mathrm{eq}}\\in\\mathcal F$ and $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x^{\\mathrm{eq}})\\ge S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(x)$ for every $x\\in\\mathcal F$, using maximum in the sense of [[def-max-min]]. The finite sum is as in [[def-finite-sum]], and the summands are those of the separated macroscopic constituents under [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]]. Whenever the stable-equilibrium label is used, attainment is assumed: at least one maximizing endpoint lies in $\\mathcal F$. If $\\mathcal F$ is empty or the entropy supremum is not attained, this item supplies no equilibrium state.\n\n“Global” refers to every endpoint in this exact $\\mathcal F$, not just nearby states, states reachable by a restricted short-time dynamics, or states on a selected branch. A local maximum that is not global, or a long-lived preparation trapped by kinetic barriers, is not declared stable equilibrium by this postulate. No uniqueness or relaxation dynamics is assumed.",
        "deps": [
          "def-finite-sum",
          "def-max-min",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
          "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set",
          "def-td-thermal-contact-constraints",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-finite-sum": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-max-min": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-td-thermal-contact-constraints": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-td-bulk-additivity": "formulation-prerequisite"
        },
        "provenance": {
