            }
          ]
        },
        "dependency_level": 6,
        "inventory_expansion_required": false,
        "library": "physics",
        "physical_scope": "Global stable equilibrium of an explicitly specified isolated macroscopic compound under its stated total-coordinate constraints and permitted exchanges; no claim about local maxima or dynamical relaxation is included."
      },
      {
        "id": "post-td-macroscopic-second-law",
        "kind": "postulate",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Macroscopic second law for isolated compounds",
        "statement": "For every admitted closed process record $P\\in\\mathcal P_{\\mathrm{cl}}(s)$ of an isolated compound, assume its declared outer boundary is isolated throughout, its material constitution is fixed, its center-of-mass kinetic and separately tracked external-field potential energies have zero endpoint change, and both endpoint preparations are equilibrium macrostates. Include every named auxiliary and work device in the compound. Fix the finite constituent split $s_1,\\ldots,s_m$ used for the endpoint bookkeeping, with $m\\in\\mathbb N$, $m\\ge1$, and constitutions $c_1,\\ldots,c_m$. The split accounts for every constituent whose entropy can change; any included auxiliary not represented in it must have an explicitly model-declared zero entropy change between these endpoints. Its endpoint product is\n\n$$\\mathcal D:=D_{c_1}\\times\\cdots\\times D_{c_m},\\qquad \\mathcal F\\subseteq\\mathcal D,$$\n\nwhere $\\mathcal F$ is the model-declared feasible endpoint set for the fixed totals, retained barriers, and permitted exchanges, as in [[def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set]]. The process model supplies an endpoint association on the two formal endpoint labels $-$ and $+$:\n\n$$\\rho_P:\\{-,+\\}\\longrightarrow\\mathcal F,\\qquad \\rho_P(-)=\\mathbf x_P^-=(x_{1,P}^-,\\ldots,x_{m,P}^-),\\qquad \\rho_P(+)=\\mathbf x_P^+=(x_{1,P}^+,\\ldots,x_{m,P}^+),$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf x_P^-$ and $\\mathbf x_P^+$ are respectively the declared constituent-state tuples representing the aggregate equilibrium endpoints $x_P^-$ and $x_P^+$ of $P$. Both tuples must belong to the same $\\mathcal F$. Restrict to endpoint splits admitted by [[post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy]] as a collection of independent separated macroscopic systems, so that its additive entropy rule applies. On this admitted $\\mathcal F$, define\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\mathbf x):=\\sum_{i=1}^{m}S_{c_i}(x_i)\\qquad\\left(\\mathbf x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_m)\\in\\mathcal F\\right).$$\n\nThis uses the finite-sum convention of [[def-finite-sum]].\n\nThe adopted macroscopic second law is\n\n$$S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\rho_P(+))-S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\rho_P(-))\\ge0.$$\n\nThe process record and the zero-change conditions are those of [[def-td-process-and-protocol]]. The process may pass through nonequilibrium configurations; this postulate compares endpoint entropies only and assigns no entropy function to those intermediate configurations.\n\nFor an admitted reversible operation in the sense of [[def-td-reversible-process]], adopt $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\rho_P(+))-S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\rho_P(-))=0$. An admitted irreversible adiabatic operation may have a strictly positive entropy increase, but strict increase is not required for every irreversible operation. The postulate does not state monotonicity for an open compound, an externally driven process, a subsystem that exchanges energy with an omitted environment, or every microscopic trajectory.",
        "deps": [
          "post-td-concave-fundamental-entropy",
          "def-td-system-and-permitted-exchanges",
          "def-td-process-and-protocol",
          "def-td-reversible-process",
          "def-td-isolated-compound-feasible-set",
          "def-finite-sum"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
