        "id": "ex-td-irreversible-adiabatic-compression",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Entropy increase in an idealized adiabatic compression",
        "statement": "This is a conditional model calculation, not a report of an observed compression. The gas is taken to have one mole, $n=1\\,\\mathrm{mol}$, of a model whose state variables are a positive temperature $T$ in kelvins, volume $V$ in cubic metres, and uniform pressure $p$ in pascals. The model is monatomic and ideal in the explicit sense that its molar constant-volume heat capacity is $C_V=3R/2$, its internal energy is $U=nC_VT$, and its equation of state is $pV=nRT$. Its entropy difference is\n\n$$S(T,V)-S(T_0,V_0)=nC_V\\ln(T/T_0)+nR\\ln(V/V_0),$$\n\nfor positive reference values $T_0$ in K and $V_0$ in $\\mathrm{m^3}$. At initial equilibrium let $T_i>0$ and $V_i>0$. A piston protocol suddenly raises the external pressure from the initial value $p_i$ to a constant final load $p_f$, compresses the gas adiabatically to $V_f=(9/10)V_i$, and dissipatively settles it to a final equilibrium state. The protocol admits no reverse record, so it is irreversible by [[def-td-reversible-process]]. The idealization assumes no change in the included load's entropy; all numerical behavior is a model assumption and deduction, not an empirical measurement.",
        "deps": [
          "def-td-process-and-protocol",
          "def-td-reversible-process",
