      {
        "id": "ex-td-noncompact-entropy-supremum-not-attained",
        "kind": "example",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "A concave entropy supremum not attained on an open energy-partition set",
        "statement": "Take two independent separated one-species systems $A$ and $B$ with material constitutions $c_A,c_B$ inside an isolated compound. Write $D_A:=D_{c_A}$ and $D_B:=D_{c_B}$ for their equilibrium domains; each is $(0,\\infty)^3$ in the dimensionless coordinates $(\\hat U_i,\\hat V_i,\\hat n_i)$ of [[def-td-equilibrium-state-domain]], and each full domain is designated as an open single-phase chart. Fix positive volumes and amounts $\\hat V_A,\\hat V_B,\\hat n_A,\\hat n_B$, total internal energy $U_A+U_B=1\\,\\mathrm J$, and a thermal-contact protocol that permits internal energy transfer while keeping volumes and amounts fixed, as in [[def-td-thermal-contact-constraints]]. The admitted separated-pair set $\\mathcal C_{AB}$ of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] includes every pair in the following feasible set:\n\n$$\\phi(x)=((x,\\hat V_A,\\hat n_A),(1-x,\\hat V_B,\\hat n_B)),\\qquad \\mathcal F=\\{\\phi(x):0<x<1\\}\\subseteq\\mathcal C_{AB}\\subseteq D_A\\times D_B.$$\n\nThus $x=\\hat U_A=U_A/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$ and $1-x=\\hat U_B=U_B/(1\\,\\mathrm J)$. With $s_*=1\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}$, adopt the toy constitutive entropies $S_A=2s_*\\hat U_A$ and $S_B=s_*\\hat U_B$. They are finite, continuous, affine (hence concave), $C^2$, and have positive $S_U$ on the stated open domains. For each $\\lambda>0$, use the same formula in the own coordinates of the distinct tags $c_A^{(\\lambda)}$ and $c_B^{(\\lambda)}$. Then the coordinate scaling $\\delta_\\lambda$ of [[post-td-bulk-additivity]] gives $S_{c_A^{(\\lambda)}}(\\delta_\\lambda x_A)=\\lambda S_A(x_A)$ and $S_{c_B^{(\\lambda)}}(\\delta_\\lambda x_B)=\\lambda S_B(x_B)$. Their additive total on $\\mathcal F$ is $S_{\\mathrm{tot}}(\\phi(x))=s_*(1+x)$. The model-declared feasible set is an open energy-partition interval, so its entropy has a finite supremum but no attained maximum. This is a conditional model example, not a claim about a real material.",
        "deps": [
